.Dd October 8, 2026
.Dt WBINDKEYS 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm wbindkeys
.Nd bind keys, mouse buttons and scroll events to commands on Wayland
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl d
.Nm
.Cm permissions
.Fl -check | -set
.Nm
.Cm service
.Fl -install | -uninstall
.Nm
.Cm record
.Op Fl o Ar file
.Nm
.Cm man
.Op Fl -install
.Nm
.Fl h | V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs shell commands when key combinations, mouse buttons or scroll events
are pressed.
It is a Wayland replacement for
.Xr xbindkeys 1 ,
configured with a Lua script.
.Pp
Input is read directly from the input devices in
.Pa /dev/input
through libinput rather than through the compositor, so it works the same
under any Wayland compositor.
Because of this, the user running
.Nm
needs read access to those devices; see
.Sx PERMISSIONS
below.
.Pp
Run without a subcommand,
.Nm
loads its configuration and keeps running, listening for input.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d , Fl -debug
Print log output to stderr: which input devices were opened (or could not
be opened), the configuration file being loaded, each registered binding and
the key codes it maps to, the keyboard layout, every key press with its
combination, and whether it matched a binding.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print a summary of options and exit.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm permissions Fl -check
Check that the udev rule is installed and that every input device can be
read by the current user.
Run it as your normal user, not as root.
Lists any devices that cannot be read and exits with status 1 if anything is
wrong.
.It Cm permissions Fl -set
Install the udev rule in
.Pa /etc/udev/rules.d/69-wbindkeys.rules ,
reload udev rules and re-trigger input devices so the current session gets
access.
Must be run as root.
.It Cm service Fl -install
Install
.Nm
as a service that starts when you log in, using the service manager the
system runs, then enable and (re)start it.
The service runs the binary this command was invoked as, so run it again
after moving the binary.
Run it as your normal user, not as root.
.Pp
Only
.Xr systemd 1
on Linux is supported for now, where this writes a user unit to
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/systemd/user/wbindkeys.service .
On other systems it prints that the system is not supported yet and exits
with status 1.
.It Cm service Fl -uninstall
Stop and disable the service and remove what
.Cm service Fl -install
set up.
.It Cm record Op Fl o Ar file
Record every key press, mouse button and scroll that
.Nm
sees, what it made of each one and which binding it would run, until
.Sy Ctrl+C .
Use it when a key combination doesn't do what you expect, and attach the
file to a bug report.
.Pp
The recording is written to
.Pa wbindkeys-recording.txt ,
or to the file given with
.Fl o , Fl -output .
It starts with the
.Nm
version, the system, the desktop, the keyboard layout settings, the input
devices and the bindings in your configuration (without their commands),
followed by one line per event.
Everything typed while recording is saved, so don't type passwords.
.It Cm man Op Fl -install
Print this manual page.
With
.Fl -install ,
write it to
.Pa ../share/man/man1/wbindkeys.1
relative to the
.Nm
binary instead, e.g.\&
.Pa ~/.cargo/share/man/man1
after
.Ic cargo install
or
.Pa ~/.local/share/man/man1
after
.Ic make install .
.Xr man 1
looks there automatically for programs in
.Pa ~/.cargo/bin
or
.Pa ~/.local/bin
on your
.Ev PATH ,
so no
.Ev MANPATH
changes are needed.
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION
The configuration is a Lua script.
Bindings are created with the
.Fn bind
function, which maps a key combination to a shell command:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bind("ALT+T", "alacritty")
bind("MOD+Mouse4", "wofi --show drun")
bind("ALT+ScrollUp", "pactl set-sink-volume @DEFAULT_SINK@ +5%")
.Ed
.Pp
Commands are run with
.Xr sh 1
and their output is discarded.
.Pp
A binding runs as soon as its combination is pressed.
An optional third argument changes when it runs:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li { on = \(dqrelease\(dq }
Run once every key of the combination has been released, provided no other
key was pressed in between.
Use it for commands that send key presses of their own, such as
.Xr ydotool 1 ,
so the keys still held for the combination aren't added to them, or to bind
a modifier tapped on its own without running it for every combination that
uses that modifier.
.It Li { on = \(dqpress\(dq }
Run as soon as the combination is pressed; the default.
.El
.Pp
A combination can have both a press and a release binding.
For example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bind("ALT+1", "ydotool key 125:1 2:1 2:0 125:0", { on = "release" })
bind("MOD", "wofi --show drun", { on = "release" })
.Ed
.Pp
Key names are case-insensitive and joined with
.Sq + .
The available names are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Characters
Any character the keyboard layout can type: A to Z, 0 to 9, &, _, @ and so
on.
PLUS is the + key, since + separates keys.
.It Modifiers
ALT, CTRL, SHIFT, MOD (the Super key), ALTGR, SPACE, and the one-sided
LEFTALT, LEFTCTRL, LEFTSHIFT, LEFTMOD, RIGHTALT, RIGHTCTRL, RIGHTSHIFT,
RIGHTMOD.
ALT, CTRL, SHIFT, MOD and ALTGR match every key that acts as that modifier,
on either side of the keyboard; the LEFT and RIGHT names match only that
side.
If a combination matches more than one binding, the one-sided binding wins.
.It Letters, numbers and function keys
A to Z, 0 to 9, F1 to F12.
.It Navigation
UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT, HOME, END, PAGEUP, PAGEDOWN, INSERT, DELETE.
.It Other keys
ESCAPE, ENTER, BACKSPACE, TAB, BACKTICK, COMMA, PERIOD, SLASH, BACKSLASH,
SEMICOLON, QUOTE, DASH, EQUAL, LEFTSQUARE, RIGHTSQUARE.
.It Mouse
MOUSE1 to MOUSE10, SCROLLUP, SCROLLDOWN, SCROLLLEFT, SCROLLRIGHT.
.It Keysyms
Any other xkb keysym name, such as XF86AudioMute.
.El
.Pp
Keys follow the keyboard layout: a binding means the keys labelled with it,
so on a French keyboard ALT+A is the key labelled A.
A character that needs Shift or AltGr adds it to the binding: on a US
keyboard MOD+& is Super+Shift+7.
With several layouts, characters are looked up in the first one, then the
others.
Modifiers can be pressed in any order.
A key name the layout doesn't have is an error at startup.
.Pp
The layout is the one the compositor uses.
To choose one yourself, call
.Fn keyboard
with the layout names
.Xr localectl 1
uses:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
keyboard{ layout = "fr" }
keyboard{ layout = "us,se", options = "caps:swapescape" }
.Ed
.Pp
It takes
.Cm layout ,
.Cm variant
and
.Cm options .
Without it and without a compositor,
.Nm
uses the US layout.
Changes to the layout take effect when
.Nm
restarts.
.Sh PERMISSIONS
.Nm
runs as a normal user and needs read access to
.Pa /dev/input .
Running
.Ic sudo wbindkeys permissions --set
once installs a udev rule that tags input devices with
.Cm uaccess ,
so
.Xr systemd-logind 8
grants access to the user in the active local session.
Check access with
.Ic wbindkeys permissions --check .
.Pp
If devices are still not readable, re-run
.Cm permissions Fl -set ,
or log out and back in.
Access is only granted to the session at the physical seat, so it does not
work over SSH.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Directory the configuration file is read from; see
.Sx FILES .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wbindkeys/init.lua
Configuration file.
Defaults to
.Pa ~/.config/wbindkeys/init.lua
when
.Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is not set.
.Nm
exits with an error if it does not exist.
.It Pa /etc/udev/rules.d/69-wbindkeys.rules
udev rule installed by
.Cm permissions Fl -set .
.It Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/systemd/user/wbindkeys.service
systemd user unit written by
.Cm service Fl -install
on Linux.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
Success, or
.Cm permissions Fl -check
found no problems.
.It 1
.Cm permissions Fl -check
found a problem,
.Cm permissions Fl -set
failed,
.Cm service
failed or is not supported on this system, or
.Cm man Fl -install
could not write the page.
.It 2
Invalid command line arguments.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Debug a configuration (stop the service first so it doesn't also react):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
systemctl --user stop wbindkeys.service
wbindkeys --debug
.Ed
.Pp
Grant access to input devices when
.Nm
is in
.Pa ~/.local/bin :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sudo ~/.local/bin/wbindkeys permissions --set
.Ed
.Pp
Check access:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
wbindkeys permissions --check
.Ed
.Pp
Start
.Nm
automatically when you log in:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
wbindkeys service --install
.Ed
.Pp
Record what happens when a key combination doesn't work, to attach to a bug
report:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
wbindkeys record
.Ed
.Pp
Install this manual page after
.Ic cargo install wbindkeys :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
wbindkeys man --install
man wbindkeys
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xbindkeys 1 ,
.Xr libinput 4 ,
.Xr systemd.unit 5 ,
.Xr udev 7 ,
.Xr systemd-logind 8
